---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Clipboard Fixture Capture"
  description="Zero-build browser tool for rich clipboard debugging. Capture browser-visible clipboard payloads from rendered chat and web surfaces, then open a prefilled GitHub issue."
  currentNav="tools"
>
  <div class="page-fade">
    <article class="article-shell">
      <a class="back" href="/tools/">← Back to AI Tools</a>
      <div class="a-meta">
        <span>tool</span>
        <span>clipboard</span>
        <span>rich paste</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Clipboard Fixture Capture</h1>
      <p class="a-lede">
        Zero-build browser tool for rich clipboard debugging. Capture the real browser-visible payloads behind rendered answers from Claude, ChatGPT, GitHub, Notion, and similar sources, then open a prefilled GitHub issue with the full fixture packet copied to the clipboard.
      </p>

      <div class="a-mirrors">
        <a class="pill solid" href="/tools/clipboard-capture/">Open the tool →</a>
        <a class="pill" href="https://github.com/xronocode/mark/tree/main/tools/clipboard-capture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub ↗</a>
        <a class="pill" href="https://github.com/xronocode/mark" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mark ↗</a>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Rendered chat output is one of the messiest real-world inputs for Markdown editors.
          The user copies something that looks correct in Claude or ChatGPT, pastes it into the editor,
          and suddenly inline code, nested lists, code fences, or tables come back mangled.
        </p>

        <p>
          This tool exists to stop debugging by screenshots and vague descriptions. Instead of asking someone
          to “show what was in the clipboard”, you give them one static page: open the tool, paste the copied
          content, inspect the payloads, then open a GitHub issue with the fixture already attached.
        </p>

        <h2>What it captures</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Advertised clipboard MIME types</li>
          <li><code>text/html</code></li>
          <li><code>text/plain</code></li>
          <li><code>text/markdown</code></li>
          <li><code>text/x-markdown</code></li>
          <li><code>text/rtf</code> and <code>application/rtf</code> when the browser exposes them</li>
          <li>Custom string formats such as <code>vscode-editor-data</code></li>
          <li>File and clipboard-item metadata</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What it does not capture</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Raw OS-native clipboard envelopes such as Windows <code>CF_HTML</code></li>
          <li>App-private clipboard formats that browsers do not expose</li>
          <li>Binary file bodies</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          That is intentional. For browser and webview paste bugs, the browser-visible payload is the first
          layer that matters.
        </p>

        <h2>GitHub issue handoff</h2>
        <p>
          The tool is not only a viewer. After a capture, it can open a prefilled GitHub issue for
          <code>owner/repo</code>, summarize the payload types and compact previews in the issue body,
          and copy the full fixture packet to the clipboard for immediate paste into the issue.
        </p>

        <p>
          That avoids browser URL-length limits while still giving maintainers a one-click reporting path.
        </p>

        <h2>Where we use it</h2>
        <p>
          Originally built for <a class="link" href="https://github.com/xronocode/mark" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mark</a>,
          specifically to debug Smart Paste fidelity from AI chats into a Markdown editor. It is also useful for
          any workflow where browser clipboard structure matters: WYSIWYG Markdown editors, rich-text import
          pipelines, browser-extension debugging, and cross-platform clipboard investigations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
